\f1\b \cf0 Why is .join() called on the separator, not the list?
\f0\b0 \
This trips up almost everyone coming from Swift. In Swift you'd write:\
industries.joined(separator: ", ")\
The list comes first, separator second. Python does it backwards:\
', '.join(industries)\
The separator comes first (because it's the object the method belongs to), and the list is the argument.\

\f1\b What it actually produces
\f0\b0 \
industries = ["Healthcare", "Legal", "Finance"]\
', '.join(industries)\
# Result: "Healthcare, Legal, Finance"\
It stitches every element in industries into one string, placing ', ' between each pair.\

\f1\b Putting it back together
\f0\b0 \
print(f"Industries: \{', '.join(industries)\}")\
# Output: Industries: Healthcare, Legal, Finance\

\f1\b Quick mental rule
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b separator.join(list)
\f0\b0  \'97 the separator "hosts" the join, the list is what gets joined.\
It's backwards from Swift, but once it clicks, it's just a rule you memorize. Want a couple of practice lines using .join()on different lists to lock it in?\
