\f1\b \cf0 Activating It
\f0\b0 \
Creating it isn't enough \'97 you have to 
\f1\b activate
\f0\b0  it so your terminal knows to use it instead of system Python:\
source venv/bin/activate\
You'll know it worked because your terminal prompt changes to show (venv) at the start:\
(venv) tatsuyamoriguchi@Tatsuyas-MacBook-Air ai-integration-lab %\
This is the Python equivalent of Xcode automatically scoping dependencies to the current project \'97 except here, "scoping" means literally switching which Python interpreter and package folder your terminal is pointing at.\

\f1\b Installing Packages (now scoped to this project only)
\f0\b0 \
pip install requests\
pip is Python's package manager \'97 think of it as the command-line equivalent of adding a Swift Package via Xcode's "Add Package Dependency" dialog. Since your venv is activated, this package installs 
\f1\b only into this project's venv folder
\f0\b0 , not system-wide.\

\f1\b requirements.txt \'97 like your Package.swift / Podfile
\f0\b0 \
Once you've installed the packages your project needs, you generate a manifest file so anyone (including future-you) can recreate the exact same environment:\
pip freeze > requirements.txt\
This creates a requirements.txt file listing every installed package and exact version, e.g.:\
requests==2.31.0\
To recreate the environment elsewhere (new machine, teammate, deployment server):\
pip install -r requirements.txt\
This is conceptually identical to sharing a Package.swift or Podfile.lock \'97 a single file that lets anyone rebuild your exact dependency set.\

\f1\b Deactivating
\f0\b0 \
When you're done working in this project:\
deactivate\
This returns your terminal to system Python. The (venv) prefix disappears.\

