\f1\b Quality-of-life tip
\f0\b0 \
If typing that activation line every time feels tedious, VS Code can auto-activate it for you: if VS Code detects a venvfolder in your project and you've selected it as your Python interpreter (bottom-right corner 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  click the Python version 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  choose the one inside ./venv/bin/python), it will 
\f1\b automatically activate the venv
\f0\b0  whenever you open a new integrated terminal in that project.\'a0\
\
\
Here's how to set it up so VS Code automatically activates your venv every time you open a new terminal in this project.\

\f1\b Step 1: Open the Command Palette
\f0\b0 \
Cmd + Shift + P\

\f1\b Step 2: Select the Python Interpreter
\f0\b0 \
Type:\
Python: Select Interpreter\
and hit Enter.\

\f1\b Step 3: Choose the venv interpreter
\f0\b0 \
A dropdown list appears showing available Python interpreters. Look for one that points into your project's venv folder \'97 it usually looks like:\
./venv/bin/python3.13\'a0 ('venv': venv)\
If you don't see it in the list, choose 
\f1\b "Enter interpreter path..."
\f0\b0  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  
\f1\b "Find..."
\f0\b0  
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  navigate to:\
/Users/tatsuyamoriguchi/Projects/ai-integration-lab/venv/bin/python3.13\

\f1\b Step 4: Confirm it's selected
\f0\b0 \
Look at the 
\f1\b bottom-right corner
\f0\b0  of the VS Code window \'97 it should now show something like:\
3.13.3 ('venv': venv)\
instead of just a generic system Python version.\

\f1\b Step 5: Test it
\f0\b0 \
Close any currently open terminal tabs in VS Code, then open a fresh one:\
Ctrl + `\
You should immediately see (venv) in the prompt 
\f1\b without typing source venv/bin/activate yourself
\f0\b0  \'97 VS Code runs that activation automatically now, every time, for any new terminal in this project.\

\f1\b Why this works
\f0\b0 \
VS Code stores this interpreter choice in a workspace settings file (.vscode/settings.json) scoped to this specific project folder. So switching to a different project (with its own venv) won't carry this setting over \'97 each project remembers its own interpreter independently, similar to how each Xcode project has its own scheme/build settings.\
