\f1\b \cf0 Part 2: JSON \'97 the big one for AI/API work
\f0\b0 \
JSON is everywhere in AI deployment \'97 API requests/responses, config files, structured data. Python has a built-in json module.\

\f1\b Writing a dictionary to a JSON file
\f0\b0 \
import json\
application = \{\
\'a0 \'a0 "company": "MSP A Co.",\
\'a0 \'a0 "role": "Sr. Solutions Engineer",\
\'a0 \'a0 "status": "Applied"\
\}\
with open("application.json", "w") as file:\
\'a0 \'a0 json.dump(application, file, indent=4)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 import json \'97 brings in Python's built-in JSON module (like import Foundation for Codable)\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 json.dump(data, file, indent=4) \'97 writes the dictionary as JSON text, indent=4 makes it human-readable (pretty-printed) rather than one long line\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Reading JSON back into a dictionary
\f0\b0 \
import json\
with open("application.json", "r") as file:\
\'a0 \'a0 data = json.load(file)\
print(data["company"])\
print(data["status"])\
json.load(file) reads the file and converts JSON text straight into a Python dictionary \'97 no Codable-style struct definition needed, though that also means 
\f1\b no type safety
\f0\b0 . You're trusting the JSON matches the shape you expect.\

\f1\b The list-of-dictionaries pattern (your job tracker, saved to disk)
\f0\b0 \
import json\
applications = [\
\'a0 \'a0 \{"company": "MSP A Co.", "role": "Sr. Solutions Engineer", "status": "Applied"\},\
\'a0 \'a0 \{"company": "MSP B Co.", "role": "Sales Engineer", "status": "Recruiter's Screening"\}\
]\
with open("applications.json", "w") as file:\
\'a0 \'a0 json.dump(applications, file, indent=4)\
This is exactly the shape you'd get back from a REST API \'97 a JSON array of objects \'97 and exactly why Python represents it as a 
\f1\b list of dictionaries
\f0\b0  in memory.\

