\f1\b \cf0 finally \'97 runs no matter what (like Swift's defer)
\f0\b0 \
try:\
\'a0 \'a0 file = open("data.txt", "r")\
\'a0 \'a0 content = file.read()\
except FileNotFoundError:\
\'a0 \'a0 print("File doesn't exist")\
finally:\
\'a0 \'a0 print("Done attempting to read the file")\
finally runs whether the try succeeded or failed \'97 useful for cleanup (though with open(...) already handles file-closing automatically, so finally shows up more in other cleanup contexts).\

\f1\b Raising your own errors
\f0\b0 \
def classify_experience(years):\
\'a0 \'a0 if years < 0:\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 raise ValueError("Years cannot be negative")\
\'a0 \'a0 if years < 2:\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return "Junior"\
\'a0 \'a0 elif years <= 5:\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return "Mid-level"\
\'a0 \'a0 else:\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return "Senior"\
raise is Python's throw. You're deliberately signaling "this input is invalid, stop and hand control to whoever's watching for errors."\
Calling it:\
try:\
\'a0 \'a0 print(classify_experience(-3))\
except ValueError as e:\
\'a0 \'a0 print(f"Invalid input: \{e\}")\

\f1\b Logging \'97 the professional upgrade from print()
\f0\b0 \
For real applications (especially AI deployment work \'97 API calls, background jobs), you don't want scattered print()statements. Python's built-in logging module is the standard tool.\
import logging\
logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\
logging.info("Application started")\
logging.warning("API response was slow")\
logging.error("Failed to connect to the API")\

