\f1\b \cf0 Defining a Class
\f0\b0 \
class JobApplication:\
\'a0 \'a0 def __init__(self, company, role, status):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.company = company\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.role = role\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = status\
Compare to Swift:\
class JobApplication \{\
\'a0 \'a0 var company: String\
\'a0 \'a0 var role: String\
\'a0 \'a0 var status: String\
\'a0 \'a0 init(company: String, role: String, status: String) \{\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.company = company\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.role = role\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = status\
\'a0 \'a0 \}\
\}\
Nearly identical shape. Key differences:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 __init__ is Python's initializer (double underscores \'97 pronounced "dunder init", short for "double underscore init")\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 No type declarations required
