\f1\b Adding Methods
\f0\b0 \
class JobApplication:\
\'a0 \'a0 def __init__(self, company, role, status):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.company = company\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.role = role\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = status\
\'a0 \'a0 def mark_rejected(self):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = "Rejected"\
\'a0 \'a0 def summary(self):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return f"\{self.company\} \'97 \{self.role\} \'97 \{self.status\}"\
Usage:\
app1 = JobApplication("MSP A Co.", "Sr. Solutions Engineer", "Applied")\
print(app1.summary())\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # MSP A Co. \'97 Sr. Solutions Engineer \'97 Applied\
app1.mark_rejected()\
print(app1.summary())\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # MSP A Co. \'97 Sr. Solutions Engineer \'97 Rejected\
Notice: every method takes self as its first parameter, even though you never pass it manually when calling \'97 Python does that automatically. This is the #1 "gotcha" for Swift devs: you'll forget self and get errors like missing 1 required positional argument.\

\f1\b self \'97 why Python makes you write it explicitly
\f0\b0 \
In Swift, self is implicit inside instance methods \'97 you can even often omit it entirely. Python takes the opposite philosophy: nothing is hidden or implicit. Every method must explicitly declare that it operates on an instance, via selfas the first parameter. This is described in Python's philosophy as 
\f1\b "explicit is better than implicit."
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b The __str__ method \'97 controlling how print() displays your object
\f0\b0 \
class JobApplication:\
\'a0 \'a0 def __init__(self, company, role, status):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.company = company\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.role = role\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = status\
\'a0 \'a0 def __str__(self):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return f"\{self.company\} \'97 \{self.role\} \'97 \{self.status\}"\
app1 = JobApplication("MSP A Co.", "Sr. Solutions Engineer", "Applied")\
print(app1) \'a0 # MSP A Co. \'97 Sr. Solutions Engineer \'97 Applied\
Without __str__, print(app1) would show something unhelpful like <__main__.JobApplication object at 0x104b2f...>. This is Python's rough equivalent of conforming to CustomStringConvertible in Swift.\

\f1\b Inheritance (same concept as Swift)
\f0\b0 \
class Application:\
\'a0 \'a0 def __init__(self, company, role):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.company = company\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.role = role\
\'a0 \'a0 def summary(self):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return f"\{self.company\} \'97 \{self.role\}"\
\
class JobApplication(Application):\
\'a0 \'a0 def __init__(self, company, role, status):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 super().__init__(company, role) \'a0 # calls the parent's __init__\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 self.status = status\
\'a0 \'a0 def summary(self):\
\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 return f"\{super().summary()\} \'97 \{self.status\}"\
super() works exactly like Swift's super \'97 accessing the parent class's version of a method or initializer.\

\f1\b A List of Objects (instead of list of dictionaries)
\f0\b0 \
Remember your capstone tracker used a 
\f1\b list of dictionaries
\f0\b0 :\
applications = [\
\'a0 \'a0 \{"company": "MSP A Co.", "role": "...", "status": "Applied"\},\
\'a0 \'a0 ...\
]\
Now, with classes, you'd use a 
\f1\b list of objects
\f0\b0  instead:\
applications = [\
\'a0 \'a0 JobApplication("MSP A Co.", "Sr. Solutions Engineer", "Applied"),\
\'a0 \'a0 JobApplication("MSP B Co.", "Sales Engineer", "Recruiter's Screening")\
]\
for app in applications:\
\'a0 \'a0 print(app.summary())\
This is a meaningful upgrade \'97 objects can carry 
\f1\b behavior
\f0\b0  (methods like mark_rejected()), while plain dictionaries only carry data.\

