The single biggest mental shift: NumPy lets you operate on 
\f1\b entire arrays at once
\f0\b0 , without writing loops. This is called 
\f1\b vectorization
\f0\b0 , and it's central to how AI code is written and why it's fast.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Setup
\f0\b0 \
import numpy as np\
np is the near-universal convention/alias for NumPy \'97 you'll see this exact import in almost every AI/ML codebase you ever read.\

\f1\b Creating Arrays
\f0\b0 \
import numpy as np\
arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\
print(arr)\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # [1 2 3 4 5]\
print(type(arr))\'a0 \'a0 # <class 'numpy.ndarray'>\
Compare to a plain Python list \'97 looks similar on the surface, but behaves very differently once you start doing math.\
\
why do you still need ',' in the array in the first line? If you use NumPy, you don't need to"," between elements of an array, do you?\
prices_np = np.array([100,200,300])\
print(prices_np * 2)\
Good question \'97 this is actually mixing up two different things: how you 
\f1\b create
\f0\b0  an array vs. how NumPy 
\f1\b displays
\f0\b0  it once created. They're unrelated.\

\f1\b The comma is required here because you're building a Python list first
\f0\b0 \
np.array([100, 200, 300])\
Look closely at what's inside np.array(...) \'97 that's [100, 200, 300], a 
\f1\b regular Python list literal
\f0\b0 . Before NumPy can do anything, Python itself has to first parse [100, 200, 300] as valid list syntax \'97 and Python lists 
\f1\b always
\f0\b0  require commas to separate elements. This has nothing to do with NumPy yet; it's just baseline Python syntax for writing a list.\
Only 
\f2\i after
\f0\i0  that list is successfully created does np.array() take it and convert it into a NumPy array.\

\f1\b Where the comma-less thing you saw comes from \'97 that's only about display
\f0\b0 \
arr = np.array([100, 200, 300])\
print(arr)\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # [100 200 300] \'a0 
\f3 \uc0\u8592 
\f0  no commas, because this is PRINTING\
This is NumPy choosing how to 
\f1\b format the output when printing
\f0\b0  \'97 a completely separate step, happening 
\f2\i after
\f0\i0  the array already exists. It doesn't mean you can skip commas when writing the array yourself.\

