\f1\b \cf0 Quick way to prove this to yourself
\f0\b0 \
Try removing the commas when creating the array:\
arr = np.array([100 200 300])\
This will throw a SyntaxError immediately \'97 Python can't even parse this as a valid list, so it never even gets to NumPy. That confirms: the comma requirement is a 
\f1\b Python list rule
\f0\b0 , not a NumPy rule at all.\
\

\f1\b The Big Difference: Element-wise Math (Vectorization)
\f0\b0 \
prices = [100, 200, 300]\'a0 # plain list\
# prices * 2\'a0 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  this DUPLICATES the list, doesn't multiply each item!\
prices_np = np.array([100, 200, 300])\
print(prices_np * 2) \'a0 # [200 400 600] \'97 multiplies each element\
This is the #1 "gotcha" moving from lists to NumPy arrays: 
\f1\b regular Python lists don't do math \'97 they do repetition.
\f0\b0  [1,2,3] * 2 gives you [1,2,3,1,2,3], not [2,4,6]. NumPy arrays fix this by making arithmetic operate element-by-element automatically \'97 no loop needed.\
a = np.array([1, 2, 3])\
b = np.array([10, 20, 30])\
print(a + b) \'a0 # [11 22 33]\
print(a * b) \'a0 # [10 40 90]\
print(b / a) \'a0 # [10. 10. 10.]\
In Swift, you'd need a manual loop (or zip + map) to do this. NumPy does it natively.\

\f1\b Common Array Creation Helpers
\f0\b0 \
np.zeros(5)\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # [0. 0. 0. 0. 0.]\
np.ones(5) \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # [1. 1. 1. 1. 1.]\
np.arange(0, 10, 2) # [0 2 4 6 8]\'a0 \'97 like Swift's stride(from:to:by:)\
np.linspace(0, 1, 5) # [0. 0.25 0.5 0.75 1.] \'97 5 evenly spaced points\

\f1\b Indexing and Slicing (familiar from Swift arrays, mostly)
\f0\b0 \
arr = np.array([10, 20, 30, 40, 50])\
print(arr[0]) \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # 10\
print(arr[-1])\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # 50 (last element \'97 Python allows negative indexing)\
print(arr[1:3]) \'a0 \'a0 # [20 30] (slice, like Swift's array[1..<3])\

\f1\b 2D Arrays (Matrices) \'97 This Is New Territory
\f0\b0 \
matrix = np.array([\
\'a0 \'a0 [1, 2, 3],\
\'a0 \'a0 [4, 5, 6]\
])\
print(matrix.shape) \'a0 # (2, 3) 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  2 rows, 3 columns\
print(matrix[0]) \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # [1 2 3] \'97 first row\
print(matrix[0][1])\'a0 \'a0 # 2 \'97 row 0, column 1\
print(matrix[:, 0])\'a0 \'a0 # [1 4] \'97 every row, column 0 (this slicing syntax is new)\
matrix[:, 0] reads as: 
\f1\b "give me all rows (:), column 0."
\f0\b0  This "all of one axis, specific index on another" slicing pattern is core to how you'll manipulate data (and later, tensors) in AI work.\
matrix = np.array([\
\'a0 \'a0 [1, 2, 3],\
\'a0 \'a0 [4, 5, 6]\
])\
print(matrix[1,1])\
print(matrix[1][1])\
Both print statement syntax are the same?\
Yes \'97 both print the same result, but they work slightly differently under the hood. Worth understanding the distinction.\

\f1\b Output for both
\f0\b0 \
print(matrix[1,1])\'a0 \'a0 # 5\
print(matrix[1][1]) \'a0 # 5\
Same result, but different mechanics.\

\f1\b matrix[1][1] \'97 sequential indexing (two separate steps)
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 matrix[1] \'97 first, grab row 1 entirely: [4, 5, 6]\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 [1] \'97 then, index into 
\f2\i that
\f0\i0  result: get element at position 1 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  5\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This works because matrix[1] returns a 1D NumPy array, and you're just indexing into it again, one step at a time \'97 same idea as how you'd access nested Swift arrays: matrix[1][1].\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 matrix[1,1] \'97 true NumPy multi-dimensional indexing (one step)
\f0\b0 \
This is NumPy-specific syntax \'97 you're passing a 
\f1\b tuple
\f0\b0  (1, 1) directly into a single indexing operation, telling NumPy "row 1, column 1" all at once, without creating an intermediate row array first.\
matrix[1, 1]\
is really:\
matrix[(1, 1)]\
NumPy recognizes tuples inside [ ] as "coordinates across multiple dimensions" \'97 this has no equivalent in plain Python lists or Swift arrays, since neither supports multi-dimensional indexing natively.\

\f1\b Why it matters (efficiency, not just style)
\f0\b0 \
matrix[1][1] technically creates a 
\f1\b temporary intermediate array
\f0\b0  (the full row) before indexing into it again \'97 wasteful for large matrices. matrix[1,1] goes directly to the exact element in one operation, which is faster and is the idiomatic NumPy way to do it.\

\f1\b Practical guidance
\f0\b0 \
For small examples like this, both work identically and the difference is invisible. But as you move into real data/AI work with large matrices, 
\f1\b matrix[row, col] is the preferred, idiomatic style
\f0\b0  \'97 it's faster, clearer about intent, and it's what you'll see in virtually all NumPy-based code you read from here on.\

