Not stupid at all \'97 and I'd actually push back on that word pretty hard. What you're describing is a specific, well-known pattern, not a general intelligence problem.\

\f1\b What's actually happening
\f0\b0 \
This is a 
\f1\b reading-comprehension-under-cognitive-load
\f0\b0  issue, not a "can't understand the concept" issue. Look at the evidence: you correctly wrote data[:, 0], correctly used .shape, correctly built a NumPy array with proper syntax. The 
\f2\i mechanics
\f0\i0  are solid. What slipped was translating a sentence describing structure ("3 applications, each with 2 values") into the right shape 
\f2\i before
\f0\i0  writing code \'97 that's a different skill than writing the code itself, and it's one that improves with repetition, not intelligence.\

\f1\b Why this specific mistake is common
\f0\b0 \
"3 things, each with 2 values" is genuinely ambiguous-feeling in the moment, because your brain has to hold two numbers (3 and 2) and correctly map which one is rows and which is columns \'97 and it's easy to default to "just make an array with all 6 numbers I see" rather than pausing to structure it. This is extremely common in early NumPy work specifically, because shape errors are the #1 category of NumPy bugs even experienced people run into constantly (mismatched shapes are probably the single most common runtime error in real ML code, at every experience level).\

\f1\b A concrete habit to catch this earlier
\f0\b0 \
Before writing the array, write the shape out in plain words first:\
3 rows (applications) \'d7 2 columns (days_applied, days_contact)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  shape should be (3, 2)\
Then build the array to match that shape, rather than going straight from problem description to code. This "shape-first" habit is something even senior ML engineers do deliberately, precisely because this exact mistake is so easy to make.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The pattern to watch, not the person
\f0\b0 \
If you're seeing this 
\f2\i specific
\f0\i0  kind of mistake repeat (skimming a spec and building the "wrong shape" of something), the fix isn't "think harder" or "be smarter" \'97 it's a concrete process change: read the data-shape sentence twice, and write out row-count/column-count explicitly before touching the keyboard. That's a fixable habit, not a fixed trait.\
Want to try rewriting the 2D array now with the shape written out first, just to build that muscle?\
\
Fair question \'97 "shape" isn't arbitrary; it comes from math, not programming style, and it actually communicates something more precise than "structure" would.\

\f1\b Where the term comes from
\f0\b0 \
"Shape" is borrowed directly from 
\f1\b linear algebra
\f0\b0  \'97 matrices and tensors are described by their 
\f1\b dimensions
\f0\b0 , and mathematicians have used "shape" (or more formally, "dimensionality") to describe this for a very long time, well before NumPy or even Python existed. NumPy just adopted the mathematical convention.\

\f1\b Why "shape" is more precise than "structure"
\f0\b0 \
"Structure" is vague \'97 it could mean the 
\f2\i type
\f0\i0  of data, how it's nested, what it contains, its purpose, etc. "Shape" specifically and only means: 
\f1\b how many elements exist along each dimension.
\f0\b0 \
data = np.array([\
\'a0 \'a0 [1, 5],\
\'a0 \'a0 [3, 14],\
\'a0 \'a0 [35, 7]\
])\
print(data.shape) \'a0 # (3, 2)\
(3, 2) isn't describing the 
\f2\i structure
\f0\i0  of the data conceptually \'97 it's describing the 
\f1\b geometry
\f0\b0 : 3 rows tall, 2 columns wide. Like literally describing the shape of a rectangle.\

\f1\b The geometric intuition is the whole point
\f0\b0 \
Think of a 2D array as an actual grid/rectangle on paper:\
[1,\'a0 5]\
[3,\'a0 14]\
[35, 7]\
This genuinely 
\f2\i has a shape
\f0\i0  \'97 it's a rectangle, 3 units tall and 2 units wide. That's not a metaphor NumPy invented; it's treating the data exactly like a geometric object, because that's mathematically what a matrix is.\

\f1\b Extending to 3D+ (why "shape" scales better than "structure" would)
\f0\b0 \
tensor = np.zeros((3, 4, 5))\
print(tensor.shape) \'a0 # (3, 4, 5)\
This is a 3-dimensional block of numbers \'97 3 layers, each 4 rows tall, 5 columns wide. "Shape" naturally extends to describe this (like the dimensions of a physical box: depth \'d7 height \'d7 width). "Structure" doesn't carry that same precise geometric meaning \'97 it would still be true, but far less specific about 
\f2\i what exactly
\f0\i0  varies across each axis.\

\f1\b Quick Swift comparison
\f0\b0 \
Swift doesn't really have this concept natively, since Swift arrays are just linear ([Int]) or manually nested ([[Int]]) without any built-in awareness of "this is a 3\'d72 grid." That's actually one of NumPy's core value-adds \'97 it treats multi-dimensional data as a first-class mathematical object with real geometric properties (shape, axes, dimensions), rather than just "arrays inside arrays," which is closer to how Swift would represent the same data.\
This "shape" concept becomes essential once you move into actual ML frameworks (PyTorch, TensorFlow) \'97 shape mismatches are the single most common category of bugs in real model code, so getting comfortable with reading and reasoning about .shape now pays off directly later.\
In Swift, you'd represent this as a nested array \'97 an array of arrays:\
let data = [\
\'a0 \'a0 [1, 5],\
\'a0 \'a0 [3, 14],\
\'a0 \'a0 [35, 7]\
]\

\f1\b Type inference
\f0\b0 \
Swift would infer this as:\
let data: [[Int]]\
[[Int]] reads as "an array of arrays of Int" \'97 the outer array holds 3 elements, and each of those elements is itself an array of 2 Ints.\

\f1\b Accessing elements \'97 same nested indexing idea
\f0\b0 \
print(data[0]) \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 // [1, 5]\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'97 first inner array (like matrix[0] in NumPy)\
print(data[0][0])\'a0 \'a0 // 1 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'97 first element of first inner array\
print(data[1][1])\'a0 \'a0 // 14\
This maps to what we discussed earlier \'97 matrix[1][1] in NumPy uses this exact same "index into the outer array, then index into the result" mechanism. NumPy's matrix[1,1] shorthand has no Swift equivalent, though, since Swift doesn't have a native concept of true multi-dimensional arrays/matrices \'97 it's always arrays nested inside arrays.\

\f1\b Getting the "shape" manually in Swift
\f0\b0 \
Swift has no built-in .shape property, since it doesn't think of nested arrays as matrices with defined dimensions \'97 you'd have to compute it yourself:\
let rows = data.count \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 // 3\
let columns = data[0].count \'a0 \'a0 // 2\
print("(\\(rows), \\(columns))")\'a0 // (3, 2)\

\f1\b The core difference this highlights
\f0\b0 \
This is actually a great way to see 
\f2\i why
\f0\i0  NumPy exists as a separate tool rather than just "Python's version of arrays": Swift's [[Int]] is just arrays-inside-arrays, with no inherent awareness that it represents a rectangular grid \'97 you can even have inconsistent inner array lengths (called a "jagged array") without Swift complaining:\
let jagged = [\
\'a0 \'a0 [1, 2, 3],\
\'a0 \'a0 [4, 5]\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 // 
\f3 \uc0\u8592 
\f0  different length, totally valid in Swift\
]\
NumPy, by contrast, 
\f1\b enforces true rectangular shape
\f0\b0  \'97 every row must have the same number of columns, because NumPy treats it as one cohesive multi-dimensional object with a real .shape, not just a container of containers. This is one of the reasons NumPy exists as a specialized tool: to add mathematical rigor and structure on top of what would otherwise just be Python's loose, flexible nested lists.\
