\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 pd.read_csv(...) 
\f1\b loads a copy
\f0\b0  of the file's data into memory, as a DataFrame\
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Anything you do afterward (df["new_column"] = ..., filtering, sorting, cleaning) only changes 
\f1\b that in-memory copy
\f0\b0 \
\ls15\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The original CSV file on disk remains completely untouched, forever, unless you explicitly save\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This is very similar to opening a Word document, editing it on screen, but never hitting "Save" \'97 the changes exist only in the open window (RAM) until you explicitly write them back to the file on disk.\
