\'a0 \'a0 df.to_csv("job_applications.csv", index=False) \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # overwrites your original file!\
I'd strongly recommend saving to a 
\f1\b new filename
\f0\b0  while you're learning/experimenting \'97 overwriting your real job-search tracking data with a mistake (like an unintended filter or dropped rows) is easy to do accidentally, and there's no "undo" once the original file is overwritten.\

\f1\b Quick verification
\f0\b0 \
After running to_csv, check your project folder:\
\'a0 \'a0 ls -la\
You should see a new file appear (e.g., job_applications_updated.csv), and if you open it, it'll now include the needs_follow_upcolumn with True/False values baked in \'97 this time actually persisted to disk.\

\f1\b Why this separation exists (and matters for real work)
\f0\b0 \
This "load 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  modify in memory 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  explicitly save" pattern is deliberate and standard across virtually all data tools (not just pandas) \'97 it prevents accidental data loss, since you have to consciously choose to overwrite something, rather than every read automatically becoming a write.\
\
\
