\f1\b Cleaning Data \'97 the "essentials" part
\f0\b0 \
Real data is messy. Pandas has built-in tools for the most common problems:\

\f1\b Handling missing values
\f0\b0 \
print(df.isna())\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # True/False table showing where data is missing\
print(df.isna().sum())\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # count of missing values per column\
df_clean = df.dropna() \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # remove rows with ANY missing values\
df_filled = df.fillna("Unknown")\'a0 # replace missing values with a default\

\f1\b Removing duplicates
\f0\b0 \
df_deduped = df.drop_duplicates()\

\f1\b Renaming columns
\f0\b0 \
df = df.rename(columns=\{"company": "company_name"\})\

\f1\b Changing a column's data type
\f0\b0 \
df["days_since_applied"] = df["days_since_applied"].astype(int)\
Ok this one returns "str": print(df_clean["days_since_applied"].dtype)\
But when do : open job_applications_updated.csv", macOS Numbers opens that file in .number extension name, and days_since_applied column data type looks double integer. Cell format indicates "Automatic" Should I open the file from other app?\
Good observation \'97 but this isn't actually a problem, and no need to switch apps. It's about how CSV files fundamentally work, not something specific to Numbers.\

\f1\b The core reason: CSV files have no real data types at all
\f0\b0 \
A CSV file is just 
\f1\b plain text
\f0\b0  \'97 commas and line breaks separating values. There's no metadata anywhere in the file that says "this column is text" or "this column is a number." Open your CSV in a plain text editor and you'd literally see something like:\
...,days_since_applied\
...,1\
...,10\
Just characters on a line \'97 nothing encoding "this is a string" vs "this is an integer." That distinction only exists 
\f1\b while pandas has the data loaded in memory
\f0\b0  (that's what .dtype was showing you) \'97 the moment you .to_csv(...), that type information is thrown away, because CSV as a format has no way to store it.\

\f1\b What's happening in Numbers
\f0\b0 \
When Numbers opens a CSV, it does its own 
\f1\b auto-detection
\f0\b0 , guessing types based on what the text 
\f2\i looks like
\f0\i0  \'97 since "1", "10", etc. look like numbers, Numbers assumes they're numeric and formats the column accordingly ("Automatic" cell format, showing as double/integer). This has nothing to do with what pandas' .dtype said before saving \'97 Numbers has no idea pandas ever called .astype(str); it's just looking at the raw text and making its own inference.\

\f1\b Is this actually a problem?
\f0\b0 \
For your purposes right now \'97 
\f1\b no
\f0\b0 . You successfully practiced the actual skill (casting a pandas column to str, verifying with .dtype) \'97 that part worked correctly and is confirmed. What Numbers 
\f2\i displays
\f0\i0  the column as, after reopening the CSV fresh, is a separate, cosmetic concern about spreadsheet software's auto-formatting behavior, not a reflection of whether your pandas code worked.\

\f1\b If you specifically want Numbers to treat it as text
\f0\b0 \
You'd need to manually change the cell format in Numbers itself (select the column 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Format panel 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Cell 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  change from "Automatic" to "Text"). But this is a 
\f1\b Numbers display setting
\f0\b0 , not something pandas or your CSV file controls.\

\f1\b The bigger lesson (useful for AI/data work generally)
\f0\b0 \
This is actually a good, concrete example of a real concept: 
\f1\b CSV is a "lossy" format for types
\f0\b0 . If preserving exact data types matters (e.g., you need "these are text codes, not numbers" to survive reliably across tools), CSV isn't the right format \'97 you'd want something like 
\f1\b JSON
\f0\b0  or 
\f1\b Parquet
\f0\b0 , both of which 
\f2\i do
\f0\i0  preserve type information explicitly. This is actually one more reason JSON matters for AI/data pipelines (tying back to Module 3 from Phase 1) \'97 it's not just "for APIs," it's also better than CSV for preserving structured type info.\

\f1\b Bottom line
\f0\b0 \
Nothing wrong with your code or your file \'97 this is just CSV's inherent limitation meeting Numbers' auto-formatting behavior. No need to switch apps unless you specifically want to override how Numbers displays that column.\
\

\f1\b Stripping whitespace / fixing string formatting (extremely common in messy real data)
\f0\b0 \
df["company"] = df["company"].str.strip() \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # remove leading/trailing spaces\
df["status"] = df["status"].str.lower()\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # normalize casing\

\f1\b Grouping and Aggregating
\f0\b0 \
print(df.groupby("status").size())\
Groups rows by a shared column value and counts how many fall into each group \'97 e.g., how many applications are "Applied" vs "Rejected" vs "Interview." This is the Pandas equivalent of SQL's GROUP BY.\

