\f1\b Module 3 a: Intro to a model framework \'97 scikit-learn
\f0\b0  
\f2\i (split into 2 sessions)
\f0\i0 \
Given your job-search focus (FDE/SE roles, API/SaaS integration work) rather than deep research, 
\f1\b scikit-learn
\f0\b0  is the better starting point \'97 it's simpler conceptually, faster to get a working result, and it's still what most "classical ML" interview questions and take-home tests reference. PyTorch/TensorFlow (deep learning) is heavier machinery you'd reach for later, mainly if you end up working with neural networks directly \'97 which is less common in FDE/SE work than knowing "can this person load data, train a model, and evaluate it."\
Given your 2-hour cap, I'll split this into two sessions (matching the revised plan). 
\f1\b This is session 3a: what scikit-learn is, loading a dataset, and the train/test split concept.
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b The Big Picture \'97 What scikit-learn Actually Does
\f0\b0 \
Scikit-learn is a library for 
\f1\b classical machine learning
\f0\b0  \'97 algorithms that learn patterns from labeled examples (this is called "supervised learning"). Unlike NumPy/pandas (which manipulate data), scikit-learn's job is to 
\f1\b learn a pattern from data, then predict on new data it hasn't seen.
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b The Swift Analogy
\f0\b0 \
There isn't really a direct one \'97 this is genuinely new territory, since iOS development rarely involves training models from scratch (you typically consume pretrained CoreML models instead). Think of scikit-learn's role like this: instead of 
\f2\i you
\f0\i0  writing the if/else rules for classification, you show the algorithm hundreds of labeled examples, and it 
\f2\i derives
\f0\i0 the rules itself.\

\f1\b Setup
\f0\b0 \
pip install scikit-learn\
pip freeze > requirements.txt\
\
import sklearn\
Note the package name mismatch: you pip install scikit-learn (with a hyphen), but import sklearn (no hyphen, abbreviated) \'97 a common early stumbling point, so worth flagging now before it trips you up.\

\f1\b Loading a Built-in Dataset
\f0\b0 \
Scikit-learn ships with several toy datasets for learning \'97 perfect for getting the mechanics down before using your own data.\
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\
iris = load_iris()\
print(iris.data[:5])\'a0 \'a0 \'a0 # first 5 rows of feature data\
print(iris.target[:5])\'a0 \'a0 # first 5 labels (what we're trying to predict)\
print(iris.feature_names) # names of each feature/column\
print(iris.target_names)\'a0 # names of each possible label/class\
The classic "Iris" dataset: 150 flower measurements (petal length, petal width, sepal length, sepal width), each labeled with which of 3 species it is. The task: 
\f1\b given measurements, predict the species.
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b Understanding the Data Shape
\f0\b0 \
print(iris.data.shape) \'a0 \'a0 # (150, 4) \'97 150 flowers, 4 measurements each\
print(iris.target.shape) \'a0 # (150,) \'a0 \'97 150 labels, one per flower\
Notice: iris.data is literally a NumPy array (everything you learned in Module 1 applies directly here) \'97 scikit-learn is built right on top of NumPy.\

\f1\b The Core New Concept: Train/Test Split
\f0\b0 \
This is the single most important idea in this whole module. 
\f1\b You never train and evaluate a model on the exact same data.
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b Why this matters
\f0\b0 \
If you show a model all 150 flowers, train it, then test it on those 
\f2\i same
\f0\i0  150 flowers, it might just be "memorizing" rather than genuinely learning the underlying pattern \'97 like a student who memorized the answer key rather than understanding the material. You need to test on data the model has 
\f1\b never seen
\f0\b0 , to know if it actually generalizes.\

\f1\b The Swift/iOS analogy that might click
\f0\b0 \
Think of it like unit testing with a 
\f1\b held-out validation set
\f0\b0  \'97 you wouldn't trust a feature just because it passed the exact scenario you coded it against; you'd want it tested against cases you didn't specifically design for.\

\f1\b Doing the split
\f0\b0 \
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\
\'a0 \'a0 iris.data,\
\'a0 \'a0 iris.target,\
\'a0 \'a0 test_size=0.2,\
\'a0 \'a0 random_state=42\
)\
print(X_train.shape) \'a0 # (120, 4) \'97 80% of data, for training\
print(X_test.shape)\'a0 \'a0 # (30, 4)\'a0 \'97 20% of data, held back for testing\
Breaking this down:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 X_train, X_test \'97 conventionally capital X, representing the 
\f1\b features
\f0\b0  (measurements)\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 y_train, y_test \'97 conventionally lowercase y, representing the 
\f1\b labels
\f0\b0  (species)\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 test_size=0.2 \'97 reserve 20% of the data for testing, use the remaining 80% for training\
\ls18\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 random_state=42 \'97 a "seed" for the random shuffling, ensuring you get the 
\f2\i same
\f0\i0  split every time you run this code (crucial for reproducibility \'97 without it, you'd get a different random split each run, making results inconsistent between attempts)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Why X and y naming convention specifically
\f0\b0 \
This comes from math notation \'97 in the equation y = f(X), X represents your inputs and y represents your output/target. This convention is universal across virtually all ML code you'll ever read, in any language or framework.\

