Run this \'97 you should see Model trained successfully followed by the model's settings, with no errors. Any questions on this step, or ready to move to Section 3 (making predictions)?\

\f1\b Section 3: Making predictions \'97 .predict()
\f0\b0 \
predictions = model.predict(X_test)\
print(predictions)\

\f1\b What this does
\f0\b0 \
Remember: X_test holds 30 flowers' measurements that the model 
\f1\b never saw during training
\f0\b0 . .predict() asks the now-trained model: 
\f2\i "based on what you learned, what species do you think each of these 30 flowers is?"
\f0\i0 \
The model looks at each flower's 4 measurements, applies the pattern it worked out during .fit(), and outputs its best guess for each one.\

\f1\b Expected output (something like)
\f0\b0 \
[1 0 2 1 1 0 1 2 1 1 2 0 0 0 0 1 2 1 1 2 0 2 0 2 2 2 2 2 0 0]\
30 numbers \'97 one guessed species per test flower, using the same encoding as before (0 = Setosa, 1 = Versicolor, 2 = Virginica).\

\f1\b Important: these are GUESSES, not the real answers
\f0\b0 \
The model has no idea if it's right or wrong at this point \'97 it's just outputting its best prediction based on patterns it learned. We haven't checked accuracy yet; that's the next section.\

\f1\b Comparing guesses to the real answers, just to look (not the formal accuracy check yet)
\f0\b0 \
print("Predictions:", predictions)\
print("Actual: \'a0 \'a0 ", y_test)\
This lets you visually eyeball how many match, side by side \'97 genuinely useful for building intuition before we do the formal accuracy calculation in Section 4.\

\f1\b The key conceptual point
\f0\b0 \
.predict() is the entire 
\f2\i reason
\f0\i0  you trained a model in the first place \'97 this is the payoff step. Everything before this (loading data, splitting, .fit()) was setup; .predict() is where the model actually does something useful: making a guess on data it's never seen.\
Try adding these lines to your script and running it \'97 paste me what predictions and y_test show side by side, and we'll eyeball how close they look before formally calculating accuracy in Section 4.\

\f1\b Section 4: Checking accuracy
\f0\b0 \
Now let's formally measure how well the model did, instead of eyeballing it.\
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\
accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, predictions)\
print(f"Accuracy: \{accuracy\}")\

\f1\b What this does
\f0\b0 \
accuracy_score() compares your two lists \'97 y_test (the real, correct species) and predictions (the model's guesses) \'97 position by position, and calculates: 
\f1\b what percentage of guesses matched the real answer?
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b Expected output
\f0\b0 \
Accuracy: 1.0\
(Iris is a famously "easy" dataset \'97 a well-tuned model often gets 100% or very close to it on this specific dataset. Don't expect this on messier real-world data \'97 this is somewhat of a best-case scenario.)\

