\f1\b \cf0 An alternative, simpler way to get the same result
\f0\b0 \
accuracy = model.score(X_test, y_test)\
print(f"Accuracy: \{accuracy\}")\
.score() is a shortcut built directly into the model \'97 it internally does .predict() and accuracy_score() together in one step. Both approaches give you the identical number; accuracy_score() is more explicit/step-by-step (better for learning), .score()is more common in real, quick-and-dirty code.\

\f1\b The full picture, all 4 sections together
\f0\b0 \
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\
iris = load_iris()\
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\
\'a0 \'a0 iris.data, iris.target, test_size=0.2, random_state=42\
)\
model = LogisticRegression(max_iter=200)\
model.fit(X_train, y_train)\
predictions = model.predict(X_test)\
accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, predictions)\
print(f"Accuracy: \{accuracy\}")\

